<section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="section-kicker">Access-price frontier</p><h2>Monthly subscription price versus direct model intelligence</h2></div><p>This answers the narrow question “what is the cheapest subscription that exposes this intelligence level?” It deliberately ignores allowance size, so evidence and allowance remain visible.</p></div><div class="panel"><div class="table-shell"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan / model</th><th>Price</th><th>Intelligence</th><th>Subscription $/intelligence point</th><th>Allowance evidence</th><th>Allowance</th></tr></thead><tbody>{access_rows}</tbody></table></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="section-kicker">Subscription allowance</p><h2>Quantified plan value versus direct model intelligence</h2></div><p>The quality-adjusted cost is a labeled heuristic. The Pareto frontier itself uses the original allowance and intelligence dimensions.</p></div><div class="panel"><div class="table-shell"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan / model</th><th>Plan value multiple</th><th>Intelligence</th><th>Quality-adjusted subsidy signal</th><th>Subscription $/quality-adjusted value $</th><th>Transfer limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>{plan_rows}</tbody></table></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="section-kicker">Directly joinable task economics</p><h2>Codex subscription cost per expected external-benchmark pass</h2></div><p>These rows join a measured plan pool to a native externally tested route; five-hour caps and shared ChatGPT usage can bind first.</p></div><div class="panel"><div class="table-shell"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan / model</th><th>Index</th><th>Attempts/avg month</th><th>Expected passes/avg month</th><th>Subscription $/expected pass</th></tr></thead><tbody>{task_rows}</tbody></table></div></div></section>'''
    write_page("/leaders/", layout("Pareto leaders", body, "/leaders/"))


def build_models() -> None:
    ordered = sorted(models, key=lambda row: (-(row.get("bestModelIntelligenceScore") or -1), row["name"].lower()))
    counts = {}
    for row in models:
        counts[row["benchmarkCoverage"]] = counts.get(row["benchmarkCoverage"], 0) + 1
    rows = "".join(f'<tr><td><strong>{e(row["name"])}</strong></td><td class="numeric">{fmt(row["planCount"])}</td><td><span class="coverage-{e(row["benchmarkCoverage"])}">{e(row["benchmarkCoverage"])}</span></td><td class="numeric">{fmt(row.get("bestModelIntelligenceScore"))}</td><td class="numeric">{"$"+fmt(row.get("bestModelBlendedPricePerMUsd"),3) if row.get("bestModelBlendedPricePerMUsd") is not None else "—"}</td><td class="numeric">{"$"+fmt(row.get("blendedUsdPerIntelligencePoint"),5) if row.get("blendedUsdPerIntelligencePoint") is not None else "—"}</td><td class="numeric">{fmt(row.get("bestCodingAgentScore"))}</td><td>{e(row.get("exclusionReason") or "Direct external evidence attached.")}</td></tr>' for row in ordered)
    missing = counts.get("no-external-benchmark", 0) + counts.get("catalog-only", 0)
    body = f'''<section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">{len(models)} advertised routes</p><h1>Every model gets a coverage record, not a borrowed score.</h1><p class="lede">Direct external results enter rankings. Broad catalogs, managed-agent labels, and unmatched aliases remain visible with an explicit exclusion reason.</p></section><section class="section compact"><div class="kpi-grid"><article class="kpi-card"><strong>{counts.get('direct-agent-and-model',0)}</strong><span>direct agent and model evidence</span></article><article class="kpi-card"><strong>{counts.get('direct-model',0)}</strong><span>direct model-only evidence</span></article><article class="kpi-card"><strong>{counts.get('direct-agent',0)}</strong><span>direct agent-only evidence</span></article><article class="kpi-card"><strong>{missing}</strong><span>explicitly unbenchmarked or broad labels</span></article></div></section><section class="section compact"><div class="panel"><div class="table-shell"><table><thead><tr><th>Model route</th><th>Plans</th><th>Coverage</th><th>Model intelligence</th><th>Blended $/M</th><th>$/M/intelligence point</th><th>Best agent index</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table></div></div></section>'''
    write_page("/models/", layout("Models", body, "/models/"))


def build_methodology() -> None:
    statuses = "".join(
        f'<div class="definition"><dt>{e(key)}</dt><dd>{e(value)}</dd></div>'
        for key, value in methodology["calculationStatuses"].items()
    )
    principles = "".join(f"<li>{e(item)}</li>" for item in methodology["principles"])
    formulas = "".join(
        f'<h3>{e(key_title(key))}</h3><pre><code>{e(value)}</code></pre>'
        for key, value in methodology["formulas"].items()
    )
    body = f'''<section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Calculation rules</p><h1>Normalize each denominator, then compare only compatible outcomes.</h1><p class="lede">The market uses dollars, token credits, model calls, work units, energy, fair-use windows, and hidden pools. Tokenmaxx preserves those units, derives quality ratios from external publishers, and uses Pareto frontiers instead of forcing everything into one score.</p></section>
<article class="prose section compact">
<h2>Evidence states</h2><div class="definition-grid">{statuses}</div>
<h2>Why provider-hidden is a result</h2><p>Some vendors publish price, models, relative multipliers, reset windows, and concurrency while withholding the numerical base pool. The completed record identifies those mechanics, lists the missing binding denominator, and excludes the plan from allowance rankings. Replacing the gap with a message-count guess would make the dashboard look more complete and make it less true.</p>
<h2>Core allowance formulas</h2>{formulas}
<h2>Two quality layers</h2><p><strong>Coding Agent Index economics</strong> use an externally published pass-rate-like composite and API cost per task. That supports API dollars per expected pass because the denominator is a task attempt and the score is interpretable as a percentage. The model, reasoning mode, and agent harness remain attached to every row.</p><p><strong>Model Intelligence Index economics</strong> use the publisher's 70% cache-read, 20% fresh-input, and 10% output blended token price. Dividing that price by Intelligence Index yields a cost-density signal per million blended tokens per index point. Intelligence Index is not a pass probability, so it is never converted into expected completed tasks.</p>
<h2>Pareto frontiers</h2><p>A configuration is non-dominated when no other imported configuration is at least as capable and also cheaper, faster, or more generous on the selected axis. Tokenmaxx publishes separate frontiers for coding quality versus API cost, coding quality versus time, model intelligence versus blended price, model intelligence versus speed, and plan allowance versus direct model intelligence.</p><p>The labeled <strong>quality-adjusted subsidy signal</strong> equals plan value multiple × Intelligence Index / 100, but the dashboard does not use that product to declare a universal winner. The meaningful result is whether a route remains on the original two-axis frontier.</p>
